  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nWindy and cloudy conditions continue in eastern KS this afternoon \nwhile an upper trough axis pushes east of the area and as a large \nsurface ridge builds in from west. The ridge will settle over the \narea tonight, allowing skies to clear and winds to become calm. This \ncreates favorable conditions for radiational cooling and very chilly \ntemperatures tonight and early Saturday. With the first freeze \nexpected for some, confidence was high enough to upgrade the Freeze \nWatch to a Freeze Warning for the northwestern half of the forecast \narea, and to continue the Frost Advisory for our remaining counties \nwhere temperatures will drop to near freezing. There could be some \nisolated locations, especially those in valleys, within the Advisory \narea that also drop to or just below freezing for a few hours before \nsunrise tomorrow.\n\nHigh pressure begins moving east of the area tomorrow but the \nassociated cool and dry air mass will stick around. Highs tomorrow \nafternoon will run about ten degrees below average, capping temps \naround 60 degrees. Southwesterly flow and warming 850mb temperatures \nwill help nudge highs into the mid and upper 60s on Sunday. A \nwarming trend will continue into the middle of next week with each \nday about four to five degrees warmer than the previous day. While a \nfew shortwaves in southwest flow aloft may move over western KS \nperiodically this week, have kept POPs less than 10 percent until \nthe very end of the week when a strong system approaches the area.",
